ed crystalloids versus isotonic saline in critically ill patients: systematic review and meta-analysis. (2018), J Intensive Care</td></tr><tr><th>Identifier</th><td>PMID 30140441</td></tr><tr><th>Open access</th><td>True</td></tr><tr><th>URL</th><td>https://doi.org/10.1186/s40560-018-0320-x</td></tr></table><h4>Scope match (is this the same question?)</h4><p><strong>✓ same question.</strong> Intervention level: topic is class-level, comparator is class-level (match: True); population match: True. same-question comparator (matching intervention level and population) <span class='muted'>Decided by one uniform rule applied to every topic before the k was seen.</span></p><p><strong>Comparator resolution.</strong> Design-key update: our primary pool is now k=2 after refusing reconstructed non-parallel designs without an explicit design adjustment (SMART, SALT, SPLIT). Any earlier same-scope k/parity note is superseded for this build; refused trials remain screened-in eligible records but are named exclusions, not pooled counts.</p><p>Legacy comparator extraction, shared_k: not exactly verifiable (comparator trial table not machine-exposed).</p><h3>Comparator panel</h3><p>MEASURED: overlaps use the live trial families for each outcome and strand. Unknown endpoint compatibility is not counted as compatible.</p><article data-comparator='30140441'><h4>Balanced crystalloids versus isotonic saline in critically ill patients: systematic review and meta-analysis. (2018); J Intensive Care; DOI 10.1186/s40560-018-0320-x; PMID 30140441</h4><p>Registered comparator identity; unextracted quantities and trial membership remain unknown.</p><p>HELD: cache/balanced-crystalloids-vs-saline-mortality/comparator_fulltext.txt; SHA-256 d49ee735ce7d94b2da0ec89ffcf30dfde140b9064408882040266f290b84aa54</p><dl><dt>k</dt><dd>NOT EXTRACTED from held text</dd><dt>effect</dt><dd>NOT EXTRACTED from held text</dd><dt>ci</dt><dd>NOT EXTRACTED from held text</dd><dt>i2</dt><dd>NOT EXTRACTED from held text