venance — not a completed systematic search.</strong> The registry-first (AACT) adapter status for this topic is <strong>RAN_OK</strong>; its evidence set was assembled by TITLE-SEEDED RETRIEVAL (title/name-anchored queries for pre-identified trials), which cannot discover an unknown eligible trial. A query seeded with the names of known trials is not a systematic search. We retract any claim of a registry-first or systematic search for this topic.</div><h4>Positive-control recovery (NOT systematic-review recall)</h4><p>Positive-control recovery: the committed registry query re-found <strong>0/1</strong> of this topic's PRE-SPECIFIED known trials (pooled + declared positive controls; enumerated 115; status RAN_OK). Reachable ceiling <strong>1/1</strong>: 1 trial(s) are registered but not enumerated by the committed query (registry vocabulary limit — improvable). <span class='muted'>Missed: 19671655.</span> <span class='muted'>Measured 2026-09-11T23:39:14Z.</span> <strong>This is not systematic-review recall.</strong> It measures whether the committed registry query re-finds the trials ALREADY KNOWN to the build; a trial that was never in the known set is not in the denominator, so a high value does <strong>not</strong> mean the search is complete ? external audits found eligible trials (J-EMPHASIS-HF, an eplerenone trial, for the MRA topic published under the identifier spironolactone-hfref-mortality, PHILO for ticagrelor) entirely absent precisely because they were never in a known set. True recall needs an INDEPENDENTLY-GENERATED reference universe (concept query + registry enumeration, not the seed list); that rebuild is in progress. Recovery is also search REACH, not inclusion — whether a recovered trial is eligible/poolable is the screen's and extractor's job.</p><h4>Registry landscape &amp; unpublished evidence (AACT)</h4><p>Of <strong>115</strong> registry records matching the query (broad — reach, not precision): 42 have a linked publication; <strong>26</str