py for the treatment of acute venous thromboembolism and was associated with significantly less bleeding (Funded by Pfizer and Bristol-Myers Squibb; ClinicalTrials.gov number, NCT00643201).</span><br><span class='muted'>basis: SIGNAL_SPURIOUS: source span: &quot;Rates of other adverse events were similar in the two groups. CONCLUSIONS: A fixed-dose regimen of apixaban alone was noninferior to conventional therapy for the treatment of acute venous thromboembo...&quot;; Generic adverse-event narrative does not report discontinuation due to adverse events; broad term matching is spurious for this endpoint.</span><br><span class='muted'>completeness: eligible+completed+results_available</span><br><span class='muted'>completeness basis: CT.gov status/results dates from local AACT snapshot</span><br><code>RETRIEVED_REFUSED_WITH_REASON</code><br><span class='muted'>harm span: Rates of other adverse events were similar in the two groups.</span></td></tr></table></section><section class="tab" id="tab-comparator"><h3 class="tabname">Comparator</h3><table class='kv'><tr><th>Published comparator</th><td>Direct oral anticoagulants compared with vitamin K antagonists for acute venous thromboembolism: evidence from phase 3 trials. (2014), Blood</td></tr><tr><th>Identifier</th><td>PMID 24963045</td></tr><tr><th>Open access</th><td>True</td></tr><tr><th>URL</th><td>https://doi.org/10.1182/blood-2014-04-571232</td></tr></table><h4>Scope match (is this the same question?)</h4><p><strong>✓ same question.</strong> Intervention level: topic is class-level, comparator is class-level (match: True); population match: True. same-question comparator (matching intervention level and population) <span class='muted'>Decided by one uniform rule applied to every topic before the k was seen.</span></p><p><strong>Comparator resolution.</strong> van Es et al. 2014 (PMID 24963045) is a scope-matched open-access pooled analysis of the six phase-3 acute symptomatic VTE DOAC-vs-VKA trials and reports recurrent VTE including VTE-related death as RR 0.90 (0.77-1.06). van der Hulle et al. 2014 (PMID 24330006) was checked first because it is the canonical JTH systematic review/meta-analysis, but Unpaywall reported open_access=false during the build, so it was not used as the gate comparator. The trial pool now declares the canonical endpoint as symptomatic recurrent VTE (DVT / nonfatal PE / fatal PE or VTE-related death) while retaining the trial-reported component phrases and literal analysis sets. The mixed trial-reported ratio measures are displayed as a pooled first-event ratio (five HRs and one RR). The EINSTEIN placebo extension embedded in PMID 21128814 is explicitly excluded as a wrong-comparator substudy.</p><h3>Comparator panel</h3><p>MEASURED: overlaps use the live trial families for each outcome and strand. Unknown endpoint compatibility is not counted as compatible.</p><article data-comparator='24963045'><h4>Direct oral anticoagulants compared with vitamin K antagonists for acute venous thromboembolism: evidence from phase 3 trials. (2014); Blood; DOI 10.1182/blood-2014-04-571232; PMID 24963045</h4><p>Registered comparator identity; unextracted quantities and trial membership remain unknown.</p><p>HELD: cache/doac-vte-recurrence/records.json#comparator_fulltext; SHA-256 5a8887072f40d8841e56e7eb4edfe328c06e7b4a2a6ded5c4eeb16b32afb8b52</p><dl><dt>k</dt><dd>NOT EXTRACTED from held text</dd><dt>effect</dt><dd>NOT EXTRACTED from held text</dd><dt>ci</dt><dd>NOT EXTRACTED from held text</dd><dt>i2</dt><dd>NOT EXTRACTED from held text</dd><dt>pi</dt><dd>NOT EXTRACTED from held text</dd><dt>method</dt><dd>NOT EXTRACTED from held text</dd></dl><p>Trial set NOT ENUMERATED; overlap unknown.</p></article></section><section class="tab" id="tab-riskofbias"><h3 class="tabname">Risk of bias</h3><p><strong>Coverage: 6 of 6 primary-outcome pooled trials assessed</strong> (6 from registry (AACT) + trial text)</p><div class='banner'><strong>RoB spans span-checked (cross-family): 97% agreement</strong> (32 of 33 scoreable), from a seeded sample of 45 model/registry-derived domain ratings independently checked by a different model family (Fable) against each trial abstract; 12 were unscoreable (no claim, or a conservative not-stated rating). This check itself found and fixed a real error &mdash; one trial (EMPHASIS-HF) was mislabelled NON_RANDOMIZED by the registry, contradicted by its abstract; the RoB block was also visibly broken until a human review caught it. The number is here because a RoB block a reader cannot trust is worthless (<code>docs/rob_spancheck.json</code>).</div><div class='absent'><strong>Parser-confirmed contrast disclosure (per pooled trial, from the AACT arm-label parser - disclosed, not an adjustment).</strong> This measures the parser, not the trial. Eligibility should test what actually DIFFERS between the randomised arms, not the mere presence of the drug word: a trial giving the drug of interest as BACKGROUND in every arm (e.g. all arms on the same agent, randomising a different drug) is not a randomised comparison of it. For each pooled trial the randomised contrast is reconstructed from AACT <code>design_groups</code> + <code>interventions</code>: <strong>6 of 6</strong> pooled trials have a parser-confirmed contrast (the intervention of interest matched a differing coded arm). A trial with no registry arm data, or coded under a class label / development code we cannot machine-match, is shown as <em>contrast unverified</em> — a VISIBLE fail-open state, never silently treated as verified. <table class='arms'><tr><th>Trial</th><th>Contrast status</th><th>Randomised difference</th></tr><tr><td>19966341</td><td>parser-confirmed contrast</td><td title='parser-confirmed contrast: keyword &#x27;dabigatran&#x27; matched AACT arm intervention &#x27;dabigatran etexilate 150 mg&#x27;; the intervention of interest DIFFERS across the randomised arms'>dabigatran etexilate 150 mg; warfarin (inr 2-3)</td></tr><tr><td>21128814</td><td>parser-confirmed contrast</td><td title='parser-confirmed contrast: keyword &#x27;rivaroxaban&#x27; matched AACT arm intervention &#x27;rivaroxaban (xarelto, bay59-7939)&#x27;; the intervention of interest DIFFERS across the randomised arms'>enoxaparin followed by vka; rivaroxaban (xarelto, bay59-7939)</td></tr><tr><td>22449293</td><td>parser-confirmed contrast</td><td title='parser-confirmed contrast: keyword &#x27;rivaroxaban&#x27; matched AACT arm intervention &#x27;rivaroxaban (xarelto, bay59-7939)&#x27;; the intervention of interest DIFFERS across the randomised arms'>enoxaparin overlapping with and followed by vka; rivaroxaban (xarelto, bay59-7939)</td></tr><tr><td>23808982</td><td>parser-confirmed contrast</td><td title='parser-confirmed contrast: keyword &#x27;apixaban&#x27; matched AACT arm intervention &#x27;apixaban&#x27;; the intervention of interest DIFFERS across the randomised arms'>apixaban; enoxaparin; warfarin</td></tr><tr><td>23991658</td><td>parser-confirmed contrast</td><td title='parser-confirmed contrast: keyword &#x27;edoxaban&#x27; matched AACT arm intervention &#x27;edoxaban tosylate(du-176b)&#x27;; the intervention of interest DIFFERS across the randomised arms'>edoxaban tosylate(du-176b); warfarin</td></tr><tr><td>24344086</td><td>parser-confirmed contrast</td><td title='parser-confirmed contrast: keyword &#x27;dabigatran&#x27; matched AACT arm intervention &#x27;dabigatran etexilate&#x27;; the intervention of interest DIFFERS across the randomised arms'>dabigatran etexilate; warfarin</td></tr></table></div><div class='absent'><strong>Funding / conflict-of-interest disclosure (per pooled trial, from source — disclosed, not adjusted).</strong> Industry-funded trials are a documented reporting-bias dimension (they tend to report more favourable results). For each pooled trial the funding source is classified from held text (full text preferred, abstract fallback) and the registry sponsor is shown as a second source when available;